    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-181986",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times [-1,1]$ in the Cartesian plane?",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=R\\times [-1,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in [-1,1]}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}={(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/588f2574b1aaf0e96895816ca34bd9c6900a9b13342f44bd4104ad2bf4270bf5.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in R$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}={(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,1]}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in R,$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,-1)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/8458978c2766d899b03149de53739650932875d6463c25dd3bbde99bc6a71ea7.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $R:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $R,$ these line segments sweep the following bar that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/6fc81e487503ce86d3bf5366e57db087484e8bd1e8bafd52738c65e8e173c387.png)",
